// Test: what a list pattern in `??` actually does — it *compares*, it never binds.
//
// The purpose of `??` is discrimination, and `corpus/match/13_ident_scalar.zy`
// records the half of that rule nobody was testing here: an identifier in a
// pattern is a **value to compare against**, not a name being created. Every
// engine agrees — `?? [1,2,3] { [a,b,c] => a }` is `undefined variable 'a' in
// match pattern` in all three — but nothing under match/ exercised an identifier
// *inside a list* pattern, and two engines out of three quietly treated it as a
// wildcard and took the branch (DM-26, now reject/patterns/04).
//
// Nothing under match/ used `*` either, which is why `*rest` in a pattern went
// unnoticed in the browser engine for as long as it did (DM-25,
// reject/patterns/03). Binding is the destructuring operator's job, and its case
// is corpus/collections/35_rest_pattern.zy.

// An exact literal list.
r = ?? [1,2,3] {
    [1, 2, 3] => "exacto"
    _ => "otro"
}
>> r ¶

// Arity: a three-element pattern does not match a two-element array.
r = ?? [1,2] {
    [_, _, _] => "tres"
    _ => "otro"
}
>> r ¶
r = ?? [1,2,3] {
    [_, _, _] => "tres"
    _ => "otro"
}
>> r ¶

// A partial literal: fix one position, accept anything in the rest.
r = ?? [1,9,9] {
    [1, _, _] => "empieza por 1"
    _ => "otro"
}
>> r ¶
r = ?? [2,9,9] {
    [1, _, _] => "empieza por 1"
    _ => "otro"
}
>> r ¶

// An identifier inside the list is the VALUE of that variable, compared
// element-wise — this is the case no engine but the tree-walker checked.
uno = 1
dos = 2
tres = 3
r = ?? [1,2,3] {
    [uno, dos, tres] => "casa por valor"
    _ => "otro"
}
>> r ¶
r = ?? [1,2,9] {
    [uno, dos, tres] => "casa por valor"
    _ => "otro"
}
>> r ¶

// Mixed: an identifier next to a literal and a wildcard.
r = ?? [1,5,9] {
    [uno, _, 9] => "mixto"
    _ => "otro"
}
>> r ¶

// A list pattern against a SCALAR subject is a membership test, not a shape.
x = 2
r = ?? x {
    [1, 2, 3] => "pertenece"
    _ => "no"
}
>> r ¶
y = 7
r = ?? y {
    [1, 2, 3] => "pertenece"
    _ => "no"
}
>> r ¶

// The length test that `*rest` looked like it was for, written the way it is
// actually written.
xs = [1,2,3,4]
r = ?? xs$# {
    >=3 => "al menos tres"
    _ => "menos"
}
>> r ¶
